<h2>The scale of China's aquaculture feed sector, reflected directly in the trade data</h2>
<p>China takes roughly a third of India's total fish meal export value — about USD 93 million in 2024, more than any other single market. This isn't a niche or opportunistic trade; it reflects the genuine scale of China's aquaculture feed-milling industry, which needs a steady, price-competitive protein-meal supply at volumes few other buyer categories match.</p>

<h2>GACC comes before anything else</h2>
<p>Every fish meal shipment to China runs through the same gate that governs most Chinese food and feed imports: Decree 248 facility registration with GACC (the General Administration of Customs of China). This is registration of the producing/processing facility, not just a paperwork step attached to a single shipment, and it takes real lead time to complete. If fish meal for China is a new line for you, start this conversation before you're anywhere near a shipping date.</p>

<h2>Chinese buyers ask sharper technical questions</h2>
<p>Because Chinese feed mills operate at real scale and buy competitively across multiple origins, expect more detailed technical scrutiny than from a smaller market — particularly around TVN (total volatile nitrogen, the standard freshness indicator) and consistent protein/moisture specification. This isn't a barrier, just a difference in how thoroughly an experienced, high-volume buyer verifies a new supplier before committing to regular orders.</p>

<h2>Standards are set independently — confirm before testing</h2>
<p>China doesn't automatically apply the same residue and quality limits as the EU or US. Tell us China is your destination at the start of the conversation and we'll test and document specifically against Chinese requirements, rather than assuming a certificate of analysis prepared for another market will transfer.</p>

